 "article": {
  "title": "Wetlands: The Quiet Heroes of the Climate",
  "topic": "环境科普（湿地保护）",
  "level": "CET-4",
  "words": 336,
  "paras": [
   "When people talk about fighting climate change, they often mention forests and oceans. Yet some of the hardest-working places on Earth are neither. They are wetlands — the muddy, reedy lands where water and soil meet. For centuries, many people saw them as useless swamps. Today, scientists call them one of our best natural tools for a warming world.",
   "Wetlands work like giant sponges. When heavy rain falls, they soak up water that would otherwise flood towns downstream. In dry seasons, they slowly release that water back into rivers, so farms and cities keep a steadier supply. Along coasts, they do something even more valuable: they weaken storms. A wide belt of marsh can slow the waves that come with a hurricane, protecting the homes behind it.",
   "They are also powerful stores of carbon. Plants in wetlands grow quickly, die, and sink into the wet soil, where they break down very slowly. In this way, a single acre of marsh can hold far more carbon than the same area of forest. When wetlands are drained for roads or buildings, that stored carbon is released into the air, adding to the very problem we are trying to solve.",
   "Sadly, the world has lost a large share of its wetlands in the past century, and the losses continue. The good news is that restoring them is possible. Cities from New York to Shanghai are rebuilding marshes and letting rivers run in more natural paths. Such work is not always cheap or fast. But the benefits — cleaner water, safer coasts, and a cooler planet — make it one of the wisest investments a community can make."
  ],
  "zh": "一提到应对气候变化，人们常想到森林和海洋，但真正最卖力的地方之一是湿地——水与土交汇的泥泞芦苇地。它像巨大的海绵：雨季蓄水防洪，旱季缓慢放水保供水；沿海湿地还能削弱风暴、保护家园。湿地的植物快速生长、死后沉入湿土缓慢分解，因此是极强的碳储库，一英亩沼泽的储碳量远超同面积森林；一旦被排干开发，这些碳就会被释放，加剧气候问题。过去百年间全球湿地大幅流失，但恢复是可行的，纽约、上海等城市正在重建沼泽、让河流恢复自然河道——尽管不便宜也不快，其清洁水源、安全海岸和更凉爽的地球等收益，值得社区投入。",
  "hard": [
   {
    "en": "They are wetlands — the muddy, reedy lands where water and soil meet.",
    "cn": "它们就是湿地——水与土交汇的泥泞、长满芦苇的土地。",
    "why": "破折号后的同位语对 wetlands 下定义，其中 where water and soil meet 是定语从句修饰 lands。写作时可套用 “X — the ... places where ...” 给抽象概念下定义。"
   },
   {
    "en": "In dry seasons, they slowly release that water back into rivers, so farms and cities keep a steadier supply.",
    "cn": "在旱季，它们把那些水缓慢地释放回河流，于是农场和城市得以维持更稳定的供水。",
    "why": "so 引导结果状语从句，与前面主句构成“原因—结果”链。so that/so 的结果句很适合描写因果链条的现实影响。"
   },
   {
    "en": "When wetlands are drained for roads or buildings, that stored carbon is released into the air, adding to the very problem we are trying to solve.",
    "cn": "当湿地因修路或建房而被排干时，储存的碳就被释放到空气中，恰恰加重了我们正试图解决的问题。",
    "why": "When 引导时间状语从句，主句用被动；句末 adding to... 是现在分词作结果状语；we are trying to solve 是省略 that 的定语从句。分词作结果 + very 强调，是四级写作亮点结构。"
   }
  ],
